A Go source file is one tree.  Build constraints come first,
because the go command reads them only before the package clause.
The package clause is next, then the import declaration,
then constants, variables, types, methods, and functions.
Methods follow types, and free functions follow methods,
so a reader can list either sequence on its own.
A type that must stay beside its methods belongs in one block.
Extend a sequence by adding a block.
One package may contain several files.
A block with no label is part of every file.
Put a shared label to the right of a more specific one:
blocks labeled lib are part of a reference impl@@:lib.
A block that belongs to one file carries that file's whole label list.
The line break before the closing control of a block is not content,
so a file block may close on the line after its reference.
@<Go Structure@/file@=
@<.@/build constraints@>
@<.@/Internals@/package clause@>
@<.@/Internals@/imports@>
@<.@/constants@>
@<.@/variables@>
@<.@/types@>
@<.@/methods@>
@<.@/functions@>
@|Go Structure@/Filters@/format Go code@

go doc attaches the comment that ends immediately above the package clause.
Leave no blank line between that comment and the clause.
@<Go Structure@/Internals@/package clause@=
@<..@/package comment@|Text Processing@/remove ending newlines@>
package @<.@/package name@>
@

@<command@:Go Structure@/variables@+
@<.@/Flag@/command variables@>
@

@<command@:Go Structure@/functions@+
func main() {
    @<.@/Flag@/parse command line arguments@>
    @<.@/main body@>
}
@
@<Chunk@/Go Structure@/Internals@/module meta information@=
{"description": "Package name and other properties, as a Jsonnet object."}
@
@<Chunk@/Go Structure@/module properties@=
{"description": "Jsonnet fields that override the module meta information."}
@
@<Go Structure@/Internals@/package name@=
@<Text Processing@/basic python settings@>


class ModuleMeta(pydantic.BaseModel):
    model_config = pydantic.ConfigDict(extra='allow')
    name: str = pydantic.Field(pattern=r'^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*$')


meta = configuration(ModuleMeta, r'''
@<.@/module meta information@>
''')
print(meta.name, end='')
@|Basic@/evaluate@
The name field is the package identifier.
pkg stands in until a file sets its own name.
A command does not read this name; its package clause says main.
The module properties are the fields of a second Jsonnet object, which override these defaults;
further fields belong there too, and Jsonnet rejects a field given twice.
@<Go Structure@/Internals@/module meta information@=
{
    name: 'pkg',
} + {
    @<..@/module properties@>
}
@
No import declaration is emitted when the path list is empty
or contains only whitespace.
A blank line between paths starts another group,
and gofmt keeps that group.
@<Go Structure@/Internals@/imports@=
@<Text Processing@/basic python settings@>
paths = r'''
@<..@/import paths@>
'''.strip(' \t\n\r\f\v')
if paths:
    print(f'import (\n{paths}\n)')
@|Basic@/evaluate@

@<command@:Go Structure@/import paths@+
    "flag"
    "fmt"
    "os"
@
@<Go Structure@/Flag@/command variables@=
@<Text Processing@/basic python settings@>
@<.@/flag syntax helpers@>


syntax = configuration(GoSyntax, r'''
@<Invocation@/invocation syntax@>
''')
usage = syntax.usages[0]
if usage.options or usage.arguments:
    print('var command struct {')
    if usage.options:
        print('flags struct {')
        for opt in usage.options:
            print(f'{take_field(opt.name)} {opt.type}')
        print('}')
    if usage.arguments:
        print('arguments struct {')
        for arg in usage.arguments:
            print(f'{take_field(arg)} string')
        print('}')
    print('}')
@|Basic@/evaluate@
@<Chunk@/Go Structure@/Flag@/flag syntax helpers@=
{"description": "Go field names, Go literals, and the Go limits on the shared invocation syntax."}
@
@<Chunk@/Go Structure@/Flag@/usage literal@=
{"description": "Go string literal of the shared usage lines."}
@
Help that is asked for goes to stdout and exits 0;
a parse error goes to stderr, followed by the usage, and exits 2.
The usage of flag.CommandLine is silenced while parsing,
so the usage is printed once, after the stream is known.
No variable is declared here, so the main body may declare any name.
@<Go Structure@/Flag@/parse command line arguments@=
@<.@/register flags@>flag.Usage = func() {
fmt.Fprint(flag.CommandLine.Output(), @<.@/usage literal@>)
}
flag.CommandLine.Init(os.Args[0], flag.ContinueOnError)
flag.CommandLine.Usage = func() {}
if err := flag.CommandLine.Parse(os.Args[1:]); err == flag.ErrHelp {
flag.CommandLine.SetOutput(os.Stdout)
flag.Usage()
os.Exit(0)
} else if err != nil {
flag.Usage()
os.Exit(2)
}
@<.@/store arguments@>@
@<Go Structure@/Flag@/store arguments@=
@<Text Processing@/basic python settings@>
@<.@/flag syntax helpers@>


syntax = configuration(GoSyntax, r'''
@<Invocation@/invocation syntax@>
''')
arguments = syntax.usages[0].arguments
print(f'if len(flag.Args()) != {len(arguments)} {{')
print('flag.Usage()')
print('os.Exit(1)')
print('}')
for i, arg in enumerate(arguments):
    print(f'command.arguments.{take_field(arg)} = flag.Args()[{i}]')
@|Basic@/evaluate@
A short name is registered as a second flag of the same variable.
@<Go Structure@/Flag@/register flags@=
@<Text Processing@/basic python settings@>
@<.@/flag syntax helpers@>


syntax = configuration(GoSyntax, r'''
@<Invocation@/invocation syntax@>
''')
for opt in syntax.usages[0].options:
    field = take_field(opt.name)
    for name in [opt.name] + ([opt.short] if opt.short else []):
        print(f'flag.{FLAG_FUNCS[opt.type]}(&command.flags.{field}, {go_string(name)}, '
              f'{go_literal(opt)}, {go_string(opt.description)})')
@|Basic@/evaluate@
flag.Usage prints the shared usage lines, a blank line,
the help flag that the flag package defines, then the shared option summary.
@<Go Structure@/Flag@/usage literal@=
@<Text Processing@/basic python settings@>
@<.@/flag syntax helpers@>


usage = r'''@<Invocation@/command line usage@>'''
summary = f'-h, {LONG_PREFIX}help: print the usage\n' + r'''@<Invocation@/option summary@>'''
print(go_string(usage + '\n' + summary), end='')
@|Basic@/evaluate@
The generators include this chunk as one fragment, on top of the shared models,
so an option name becomes the same Go field in each of them.
The Go field is the name in camel case; hyphens and spaces separate words.
@<Go Structure@/Flag@/flag syntax helpers@=
@<Invocation@/invocation syntax helpers@>

GO_KEYWORDS = {
    'break', 'default', 'func', 'interface', 'select',
    'case', 'defer', 'go', 'map', 'struct',
    'chan', 'else', 'goto', 'package', 'switch',
    'const', 'fallthrough', 'if', 'range', 'type',
    'continue', 'for', 'import', 'return', 'var',
}


def field_name(name):
    parts = [part for part in re.split(r'[-_]+', re.sub(r'\s+', '-', name)) if part]
    if not parts:
        return ''
    return parts[0].lower() + ''.join(part.capitalize() for part in parts[1:])


def take_field(name):
    field = field_name(name)
    if not re.fullmatch(r'[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*', field):
        raise ValueError(f"name '{name}' is not a Go field")
    if field in GO_KEYWORDS:
        raise ValueError(f"name '{name}' is a Go keyword")
    return field
@
@<Go Structure@/Flag@/flag syntax helpers@+
FLAG_FUNCS = {
    'bool': 'BoolVar',
    'string': 'StringVar',
    'int': 'IntVar',
    'int64': 'Int64Var',
    'uint': 'UintVar',
    'uint64': 'Uint64Var',
    'float64': 'Float64Var',
}


def go_string(text):
    for raw, escaped in (('\\', '\\\\'), ('"', '\\"'), ('\n', '\\n'), ('\r', '\\r'), ('\t', '\\t')):
        text = text.replace(raw, escaped)
    return f'"{text}"'


def go_literal(opt):
    if opt.default is None:
        return {'bool': 'false', 'string': '""'}.get(opt.type, '0')
    if opt.type == 'bool':
        return 'true' if opt.default else 'false'
    if opt.type == 'string':
        return go_string(opt.default)
    return str(opt.default)
@
The flag package parses one usage, so a command has one usage; its positional arguments are an exact list.
A short name is a second flag, so no name may be both a short and a long name,
and h and help stay the help flags of the flag package.
Options, and separately arguments, must become distinct Go fields.
@<Go Structure@/Flag@/flag syntax helpers@+
class GoSyntax(InvocationSyntax):
    @pydantic.model_validator(mode='after')
    def check_go_usage(self):
        if len(self.usages) != 1:
            raise ValueError('the flag package parses one usage')
        usage = self.usages[0]
        flags = [opt.name for opt in usage.options] + [opt.short for opt in usage.options if opt.short]
        for name in flags:
            if name in ('h', 'help'):
                raise ValueError(f'flag {name!r} is the help flag of the flag package')
            if flags.count(name) > 1:
                raise ValueError(f'flag {name!r} is both a short name and a long name')
        for names in ([opt.name for opt in usage.options], usage.arguments):
            seen = set()
            for name in names:
                field = take_field(name)
                if field in seen:
                    raise ValueError(f"name '{name}' repeats field {field}")
                seen.add(field)
        return self
@
command is the rightmost label of a package main program.
These blocks set the package name to main,
add the flag, fmt, and os imports,
add the command variable when the invocation syntax needs one,
and add func main.
func main parses the command line and then runs the main body.
Parsing always uses the flag, fmt, and os packages,
so those three imports stay used when the command has no flags.
Replace module meta information to name the other files in the node.
That replacement does not rename the command.
@<command@:Go Structure@/Internals@/package name@=main@
